import
	../../html

intro-unions html[]()
	- section "Declaring unions",
		- p quote
			A {union_} value chooses one of several possible types. These are called the {em "case types"}.
			{br}
			Below, the case types of {u} are {nat} and {string}.
			So a {u} value must be either a {nat} or a {string}.
			{br}
			A value of a case type can implicitly convert to the union type.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = 1
				log a
				b u = "foo"
				log b

			u union
				nat
				string

		- p "Short unions can be squeezed onto one line."
		- keen-not-runnable "u union(nat, string)"

	- section "Case getters",
		- p quote
			Declaring the union implicitly creates functions for getting each of its case types.
			{br}
			In this example, those are
			{function "nat64"}{sp}{type "nat64?"}{a-u} and {function "string"}{sp}{string}{type "?"}{a-u} .
			{br}
			({nat} is an alias for {nat64}, so the case getter function uses the type's true name.)
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = 1
				log nat64 a
				log string a
				b u = "foo"
				log nat64 b
				log string b

			u union(nat, string)

		- p quote
			If a case getter is used as a condition, its argument will implicitly convert to the case type
			in the scope where the condition is true.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = 1
				b u = "foo"
				log size a
				log size b

			u union(nat, string)

			size nat(a u)
				if nat64 a
					a # 'a' implicitly converts to 'nat'
				elif string a
					# 'a' implicitly converts to 'string',
					# so this calls the 'size' function for strings
					size a
				else
					# This should never happen
					99

	- section "\"match\" on unions",
		- p quote
			{match_} works on a union value. This uses the same syntax as a {match_} on an enum value.
			{br}
			If you handle every case, you don't need an {else_} branch.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = 1
				b u = "foo"
				log size a
				log size b

			u union(nat, string)

			size nat(a u)
				match a
				as nat
					# 'a' implicitly converts to 'nat'
					a
				as string
					# 'a' implicitly converts to 'string'
					size a

	- section "Methods",
		- p "A union can list functions that must exist for all case types. These are called {em "methods"}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = 1
				b u = "foo"
				log size a
				log size b

			u union
				nat
				string
			do
				size nat()

			size nat(a nat)
				a

		- p quote
			For each case type, there must be a function matching the method,
			with the case type injected as the first parameter.
			{br}
			This is called the method {em "implementation"}.
		- p quote
			Above, the method implementations are {fn-size}{sp}{nat}{lparen}{var-a}{sp}{nat}{rparen} and
			{fn-size}{sp}{nat}{lparen}{var-a}{sp}{string}{rparen} .
			{br}
			The {nat} implementation is defined in the example;
			the {string} implementation comes from the standard library.
		- p quote
			Declaring the method also implicitly creates a {em "caller"} function
			which takes the union type as its first parameter.
			{br}
			Above, the caller is {fn-size}{sp}{nat}{lparen}{var-a}{sp}{u}{rparen} .
			{br}
			The caller matches on the union and calls the appropriate implementation.
		- aside-small quote
			Union methods let you break up a function that would have been a huge {match_} expression,
			and write separate functions for each case type.
			{br}
			Though, sometimes it is better to just write a {match_}.

		- p quote
			If the union is on one line, omit the {do_} keyword.
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			u union(nat[], string)
				size nat()


	- section "More implicit functions on unions",
		- p quote
			Unions support the same implicit functions as records:
			{fn-equal}, {fn-compare}, {fn-hash}, and {fn-to}{sp}{json}.
			{br}
			As with records, the union must be immutable to get these functions.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				n u = 10
				s u = "9"
				log n == s
				log n <=> s
				log deterministic-hash n
				log n

			u union(nat, string)

		- bullets
			- quote
				{fn-equal} returns {fn-false} for different case types.
				For values of the same case type, it calls the {fn-equal} function for that type.
			- quote
				{fn-compare} works similarly.
				When the values are of the same case type, it calls {fn-compare} for the case type.
				{br}
				When the values are not of the same case type,
				case types that were declared earlier are considered lesser.
				{br}
				If you declared the union as {union_}{lparen}{nat}{comma}{string}{rparen} ,
				{nat}s are less than {string}s.
				{br}
				If you declared the union as {union_}{lparen}{string}{comma}{nat}{rparen} ,
				{string}s are less than {nat}s.
			- quote
				{fn-hash} hashes the case type index (above {nat} is {num-0} and {string} is {num-1}),
				then calls the {fn-hash} function for the case type.
			- quote
				{to-json} returns a single-key object with the case type name as a key,
				and calls that type's {to-json} for the value.

	- section "Restrictions",
		- p quote
			Almost any types can be combined in a union, but:
		- bullets
			- "Multiple aliases to the same type (e.g. {nat} and {nat64}) aren't allowed."
			- quote
				Multiple instances of the same generic type aren't allowed.
				(These will be explained in {intro-link type-parameters}.)
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			u union(nat, nat64) # This won't work
			v union(nat[], string[]) # This won't work

		- p "You can work around that by defining wrapper types with unique names."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a u = nats (1, 2, 3)
				log a
				b u = strings ("a", "b", "c")
				log b

			u union(nats, strings)
			nats record(values nat[])
			strings record(values string[])

-a-u html[]()
	parenthesized "{var-a}{sp}{u}"
